    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "gxpmjd7",
      "content": "Punjab is one of the most historically significant regions of the Indian subcontinent. The name 'Punjab' is derived from the Persian words 'Panj' (five) and 'Ab' (water/river), meaning 'Land of Five Rivers' :the Jhelum, Chenab, Ravi, Beas, and Sutlej."
    },
    {
      "id": "13r07kg",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "1.1.1 Indus Valley Civilization"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "usgk72s",
      "content": "The Indus Valley Civilization (circa 3300–1300 BCE) flourished along the Indus River and its tributaries, many of which flowed through Punjab. Key sites located in present-day Punjab include:"
    },
    {
      "id": "b7zn1dr",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Site</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Location</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Notable Features</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Ropar (Rupnagar)</p>",
            "<p>Punjab, India</p>",
            "<p>First site excavated after independence (1953); copper tools, pottery, cemetery</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Sanghol</p>",
            "<p>Fatehgarh Sahib district</p>",
            "<p>Terracotta figurines, beads, seals</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Bara</p>",
            "<p>Near Ropar</p>",
            "<p>Late Harappan phase material</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Dholbaha</p>",
            "<p>Hoshiarpur district</p>",
            "<p>Discovered in 2000s; mature Harappan artifacts</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Chandigarh Area</p>",
            "<p>UT Chandigarh</p>",
            "<p>Harappan connections in surrounding region</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "iswcnkr",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "1.1.2 Vedic Period"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "viom8di",
      "content": "Punjab was the heartland of the Rigvedic civilization. The Rigveda, the oldest of the four Vedas, was composed mainly in the Punjab-Haryana region, particularly along the banks of the Saraswati and Sindhu (Indus) rivers. The battle of Ten Kings (Dasharajna Yuddha) mentioned in the Rigveda is believed to have taken place on the banks of the Ravi (Parushni) river."
    },
    {
      "id": "xk9m9mb",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "1.1.3 Persian and Greek Invasions"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "tn7r5fv",
      "content": "Punjab was the first Indian territory to face foreign invasions from the northwest. The key invasions were:"
    },
    {
      "id": "0srv3rz",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Invader / Period</p>",
            "<p>Key Facts</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Cyrus the Great (c. 530 BCE)</p>",
            "<p>First Persian ruler to conquer parts of Punjab; established Achaemenid rule</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Darius I (c. 518 BCE)</p>",
            "<p>Included Punjab in the 20th Satrapy; levied heavy tribute; commissioned survey of Indus</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Alexander the Great (326 BCE)</p>",
            "<p>Crossed Indus at Taxila; defeated Porus (Puru) at Battle of Hydaspes (Jhelum river); went up to Beas (Hyphasis)</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Battle of the Hydaspes (326 BCE)</p>",
            "<p>Fought between Alexander and King Porus; Porus showed great valor; Alexander was impressed and gave kingdom back to Porus</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "pst7y1c",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "1.1.4 Mauryan Empire"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "6ho12x2",
      "content": "After Alexander's retreat, Chandragupta Maurya (321–297 BCE) founded the Mauryan Empire and incorporated Punjab into his vast empire. Taxila (present-day Pakistan) was an important administrative center. Emperor Ashoka (268–232 BCE) spread Buddhism across Punjab and erected pillars and edicts throughout the region."
    },
    {
      "id": "fxhngv2",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1.2 Medieval Punjab"
    },
    {
      "id": "bdng0e9",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "1.2.1 Early Medieval Period"
    },
    {
      "id": "dfs7gih",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Period/Dynasty</p>",
            "<p>Key Events</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>White Huns (Hunas) :5th–6th century CE</p>",
            "<p>Invaded and disrupted Gupta rule; caused widespread destruction in Punjab</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Harsha's Empire (606–647 CE)</p>",
            "<p>Harsha Vardhana controlled parts of Punjab; Xuanzang (Chinese pilgrim) visited during this period</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Arab Invasion (711–713 CE)</p>",
            "<p>Muhammad bin Qasim conquered Sindh; raids extended to southern Punjab</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Pratihara Dynasty (8th–9th century)</p>",
            "<p>Gurjara-Pratiharas controlled parts of Punjab; repelled Arab advances</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Ghaznavid Invasions (997–1030 CE)</p>",
            "<p>Mahmud of Ghazni made 17 raids into India; plundered Somnath; Punjab came under his control from 1021 CE</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "jyrjqp3",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "1.2.2 Delhi Sultanate Period (1206–1526)"
    },
    {
      "type": "pullQuote",
      "content": "CHAPTER 1: HISTORY OF PUNJAB",
      "id": "o0eoafc"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "bvwssmn",
      "content": "Punjab was incorporated into the Delhi Sultanate. Key facts:"
    },
    {
      "id": "7ywzdbg",
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "Qutb ud-Din Aibak, the founder of the Slave Dynasty, was based in Lahore.",
        "Lahore served as an important administrative capital.",
        "Mongol invasions repeatedly threatened Punjab during the 13th–14th centuries.",
        "The Sufi tradition grew significantly in Punjab during this period :notably through Baba Farid (Fariduddin Ganjshakar) at Pakpattan."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "29bv6fs",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "1.2.3 Mughal Period (1526–1739)"
    },
    {
      "id": "o3oim7n",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Emperor / Event</p>",
            "<p>Significance for Punjab</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Babur (1526–1530)</p>",
            "<p>First Battle of Panipat (1526) :Babur defeated Ibrahim Lodi; foundation of Mughal Empire</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Humayun (1530–1556)</p>",
            "<p>Second Battle of Panipat (1556) :Akbar defeated Hemu (Hemchandra Vikramaditya)</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Akbar (1556–1605)</p>",
            "<p>Punjab administered through Lahore Subah; built Lahore Fort; Lahore was a major imperial city</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Jahangir (1605–1627)</p>",
            "<p>Died at Bhimber, Punjab (1627); Guru Arjan Dev Ji martyred (1606); Guru Hargobind Ji founded Akal Takht</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Shah Jahan (1628–1658)</p>",
            "<p>Lahore as a major city; construction of Shalimar Gardens, Lahore</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Aurangzeb (1658–1707)</p>",
            "<p>Guru Tegh Bahadur Ji martyred in Delhi (1675); Guru Gobind Singh Ji founded Khalsa (1699); Wazir Khan was faujdar of Sirhind</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Banda Singh Bahadur (1670–1716)</p>",
            "<p>Led Sikh uprising after Guru Gobind Singh; captured Sirhind (1710); executed by Farrukhsiyar in Delhi</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "q0rdw3m",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1.3 Sikh Period :Rise of the Sikh Empire"
    },
    {
      "id": "7cbezms",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "1.3.1 The Ten Sikh Gurus"
    },
    {
      "id": "scwcxhu",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Guru</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Period</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Key Contribution</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Important Fact</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Guru Nanak Dev Ji (1st)</p>",
            "<p>1469–1539</p>",
            "<p>Founded Sikhism; preached equality, devotion</p>",
            "<p>Born at Nankana Sahib; Guru Granth Sahib contains 974 hymns by him</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Guru Angad Dev Ji (2nd)</p>",
            "<p>1504–1552</p>",
            "<p>Developed Gurmukhi script; institutionalized Langar</p>",
            "<p>Promoted literacy; made Gurmukhi the script for Punjabi</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Guru Amar Das Ji (3rd)</p>",
            "<p>1479–1574</p>",
            "<p>Opposed caste discrimination; established Manji system</p>",
            "<p>Made langar compulsory before meeting Guru; Goindwal Baoli</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Guru Ram Das Ji (4th)</p>",
            "<p>1534–1581</p>",
            "<p>Founded city of Amritsar (Ramdaspur)</p>",
            "<p>Composed Lavan (Sikh wedding ceremony); founded Golden Temple site</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Guru Arjan Dev Ji (5th)</p>",
            "<p>1563–1606</p>",
            "<p>Completed Golden Temple; compiled Adi Granth (1604)</p>",
            "<p>First Sikh martyr; tortured by Jahangir's orders</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Guru Hargobind Ji (6th)</p>",
            "<p>1595–1644</p>",
            "<p>Militarized Sikhism; founded Akal Takht; wore two swords (Miri-Piri)</p>",
            "<p>Imprisoned in Gwalior Fort by Jahangir</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Guru Har Rai Ji (7th)</p>",
            "<p>1630–1661</p>",
            "<p>Maintained large cavalry; helped Dara Shikoh</p>",
            "<p>Focused on peaceful preaching; expanded Sikhism</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Guru Har Krishan Ji (8th)</p>",
            "<p>1656–1664</p>",
            "<p>Youngest Guru; cured smallpox victims in Delhi</p>",
            "<p>Died of smallpox at age 7; called 'Bala Pir'</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Guru Tegh Bahadur Ji (9th)</p>",
            "<p>1621–1675</p>",
            "<p>Protected Kashmiri Pandits' right to religion; martyred</p>",
            "<p>Beheaded at Chandni Chowk, Delhi on Aurangzeb's orders</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Guru Gobind Singh Ji (10th)</p>",
            "<p>1666–1708</p>",
            "<p>Founded Khalsa Panth (1699); gave Five Ks; last human Guru</p>",
            "<p>Born Patna; composed Zafarnama; declared Guru Granth Sahib as eternal Guru</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "nfom3vm",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "1.3.2 Foundation of Khalsa (1699)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "vr14igk",
      "content": "On Baisakhi, April 13, 1699, Guru Gobind Singh Ji created the Khalsa Panth at Anandpur Sahib. The five Panj Piaras (Beloved Five) who offered their heads were:"
    },
