    {
      "id": "38aoooq",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "1.3.4 Ranjit Singh and the Sikh Empire (1799–1849)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "6t4uakh",
      "content": "Maharaja Ranjit Singh (1780–1839), popularly called 'Sher-e-Punjab' (Lion of Punjab), founded the Sikh Empire with Lahore as its capital."
    },
    {
      "id": "wg9und5",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Key Facts</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Details</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Birth</p>",
            "<p>November 13, 1780 at Gujranwala</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Capital</p>",
            "<p>Lahore</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Title</p>",
            "<p>Maharaja (1801); 'Sher-e-Punjab'</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Reign</p>",
            "<p>1799–1839</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Death</p>",
            "<p>June 27, 1839 at Lahore</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Army</p>",
            "<p>Fauj-i-Ain (regular army); trained by European generals (Allard, Ventura, Court, Avitabile)</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Kohinoor Diamond</p>",
            "<p>Obtained from Shah Shuja (Afghan ruler) in 1813</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Treaty of Amritsar</p>",
            "<p>1809 :with British East India Company; Sutlej recognized as boundary</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Expansion</p>",
            "<p>Captured Lahore (1799), Amritsar (1802), Multan (1818), Kashmir (1819), Peshawar (1834)</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Administration</p>",
            "<p>Divided empire into four provinces: Lahore, Multan, Kashmir, Peshawar</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "6gxagt7",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "1.3.5 Anglo-Sikh Wars and Annexation"
    },
    {
      "id": "bad5pqu",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>War / Event</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Key Details</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>First Anglo-Sikh War (1845–46)</p>",
            "<p>Triggered by Sikh army crossing Sutlej; British won battles of Mudki, Ferozeshah, Aliwal, Sobraon; Treaty of Lahore (1846) :reduced Sikh territory, ceded Jalandhar Doab</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Treaty of Bhyrowal (1846)</p>",
            "<p>Council of Regency established; Henry Lawrence as Resident; British gained further control</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Second Anglo-Sikh War (1848–49)</p>",
            "<p>Started with Multan revolt (Diwan Mulraj) and Hazara revolt; Battle of Chillianwala and Gujarat; Sikh army surrendered</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Annexation of Punjab (March 29, 1849)</p>",
            "<p>Punjab formally annexed by Governor-General Lord Dalhousie; Kohinoor sent to Britain; Maharaja Duleep Singh pensioned off</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Board of Administration</p>",
            "<p>Henry Lawrence, John Lawrence, and Charles Mansel administered Punjab (1849–1853)</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
